vailing wind is South (F2), so if I have a Pung of F2, that's a point. But I have F4 here. Not sure if that's relevant.\n\nJ1 and J3 are in my hand. J1 is Red Dragon, J3 is White Dragon. Playing J1: there are four J1s left. J3 has three left. If I play J1, maybe others can take it. But since I have only one J1, it's safe. J3 is part of the Dragon tiles. If I keep J3, maybe I can form a Pung later if another J3 is drawn. But there are three left, so the chance is low.\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, the counts for each tile. Let's see, for example, if I play W3, which has four left. Since I have two W3s, playing one might be okay. But maybe keeping W3 could help in forming a Pung. However, there are four W3s remaining, so the chance to get two more is possible. But if I have two W3s, and there are four left, maybe it's better to keep them for a Pung. But if I discard one W3, I still have one left. But forming a Pung would require three, so even if I discard one, I need two more. It's not very likely.\n\nAlternatively, if I play B9, which is a terminal tile. B9 is part of some patterns. But since I have only one, discarding it might not be a big issue. However, terminal tiles are often valuable, so maybe holding onto them is better. But if I discard B9, others might take it for their melds.\n\nLooking at the other players' melds: the next player has a Chi of W5 (so they might have W4 and W6, but they have a meld of W4-W5-W6). The player across has no melds, and the previous player also has none. So maybe the next player is working on W tiles.\n\nIf I play W3, the next player might need it for their melds? But they already have a Chi on W5. Maybe not. So playing W3 might be safe.\n\nAnother angle: my current hand has a Chow in B3-B4-B5. To maximize points, maybe I can form more Chows or Pungs. For example, if I can get a B2-B3-B4, but I don't have B3. Or B7-B8-B9, but I have B9. If I can get B7 and B8, but I don't. So maybe not helpful.\n\nLooking at the tiles I have,